WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.033
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.901
is an Oscar and Emmy
Award-winning actress

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.667
you know from films such as
"Marriage Story"

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.267
and "Jurassic Park"
and shows like

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:08.634
"Big Little Lies"
and "Enlightened."

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.200
She is also the coauthor
of the new book

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.801
"Honey, Baby, Mine:
A Mother and Daughter Talk

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:16.067
Life, Death,
Love and Banana Pudding,"

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.634
which is now available
everywhere.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:20.267
Please welcome back to the show
Laura Dern, everybody.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:21.534
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.534 --> 00:00:29.133
♪♪

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:36.734
♪♪

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:39.434
Welcome back.
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:42.801
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.367
-I always genuinely love
talking to you.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.067
And what I love
about this book

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:49.667
is I feel like I'm talking
with you when you read it

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.868
because this is a conversation
between you and your mother.

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:54.133
And this is the kind
of conversation, I guess,

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:56.934
that you guys had never
had together before.

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.734
What was the sort of catalyst
to approaching

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:01.434
having these conversations
together?

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:05.634
-So my amazing mother,
Diane Ladd,

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:09.801
who is such an extraordinary
actor and been,

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:14.501
you know, the light of my life,
got a health diagnosis.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:17.968
She unknowingly --
In her neighborhood

00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:21.100
were surrounding farms
and they were using pesticides

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.567
and spraying
without notification.

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:27.868
And when she discovered
there was this lung disease

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.400
from scarring
from the pesticide exposure,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.634
they said "your mom likely
has 3 to 6 months to live"

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:37.734
and there was no medicine
or support,

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:42.834
so they said, so they hoped
that by walking

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:45.934
she might get more strength
and expand her lungs.

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:48.434
And so she really
didn't want to do this.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.400
Let's be clear, she said,
"I hate walking.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.100
I had to walk 10 miles
to school when I was little.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.000
Don't make me walk more."

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.400
And at the time
she was on oxygen.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.334
It was very difficult,
and she loves telling stories.

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:01.868
So I said, "I'll tell you what,
we're going to walk each day

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:03.934
a little bit further
and we're going to talk

00:02:03.934 --> 00:02:05.934
and I'm going to record it
in my phone,

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:07.567
and your grandchildren
will have it."

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:09.534
And as my mother says,
you know,

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:11.167
when you think
you're going to go,

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.634
you tell each other everything.

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:17.167
But the gift of the experience
was we discovered,

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:18.601
as close as we are,

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:21.133
and we're actors who've
worked together on films --

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:22.968
she was also
in "Enlightened" with me

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:26.467
and David Lynch's "Wild at
Heart" and "Rambling Rose" --

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:28.901
we had spoken about so little.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:31.968
We had skirted from the deepest

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:33.968
and most heartbreaking
of moments in our life,

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:36.501
and I never asked
her things like,

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:37.701
"What's the first movie
you ever saw?

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.934
Why did you want
to become an actor?"

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:43.868
And this was the discovery
of an intimacy

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.734
we don't share enough
with our closest of loved ones.

00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:50.434
-And then the real happy
ending as well is health-wise.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:51.534
She's okay?

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:53.400
-Also, to quote my mom,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.968
this occurred
at the beginning of 2019,

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.601
and she will tell you,
"Four years later,

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:01.267
I've done two movies,
a TV show and wrote a book."

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:02.534
So...

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:07.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.601
But she does believe
that the walking did in fact

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:15.367
give her more energy and that
the talking and the healing

00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:19.400
and us loving each other
was a major healing

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.634
and that she didn't
believe doctors.

00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:25.367
She was like, "They're
'practicing' medicine, Laura."

00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:27.267
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:29.367
So she's like, "I'm going to get
several other opinions."

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.601
And she believes
in alternative options

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.334
and worked very hard
to be here.

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:35.400
-Well, I'm so happy she's here.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.868
One thing that must
have been very surreal

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.834
is, so, you record her
on these walks

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:41.033
and then obviously
you transcribe it into a book

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.067
and then -- which is
a great idea, obviously,

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:45.167
because you have
a couple of actors

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:47.400
who have written a book,
you record the audiobook,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.033
so now you're back
in the booth,

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:53.033
basically rerecording a
conversation you've already had.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:54.467
Were there ever times
where you thought,

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.567
"Oh, now that I'm getting
another chance at this story,

00:03:56.567 --> 00:03:57.901
I'd love to..."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:00.267
-Yes. I mean,
you had two actresses,

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:03.267
so it felt like doing
a one-act play or something,

00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:05.367
but it was so personal.

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:08.100
And in the moment
we were sharing everything.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.200
And the only reason
we ended up doing this

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.801
was, as we started
sharing our story,

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:14.234
it was inspiring

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:17.834
friends, my children, people,
all different relationships

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:21.467
to communicate more empathically
and knowing

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:24.467
that you don't necessarily
solve anything or agree,

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:27.067
but you ask the questions
and you hear each other.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.467
But once we were in there and
we heard some of the responses,

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:33.868
there is a story about my mother
taking my son for a haircut

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:37.100
when I was out of town,
and I'm still livid.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.133
And we did the audio recording
and at the end of it I was like,

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:42.834
"You are so wrong.
I can't believe you did that."

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:45.267
And we got in a huge fight.
We needed to take five, guys.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:47.434
-It must be interesting
because the first time

00:04:47.434 --> 00:04:48.868
you're listening,
you're thinking,

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:50.634
"Well, she's only going to be
around for another three months"

00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:52.067
and now she's fine.

00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:54.834
You're like, "I got a -- I have
a different answer."

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:57.067
[ Laughter ]
-That's what she says.

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:58.634
She was like, "Wait a minute.

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:02.300
Why did I go through
that whole story with her?"

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.133
-You know, one of the things --
You guys, your first film

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:09.000
was a film your mother was in,
and Martin Scorsese directed it.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.000
And an interesting thing
is your mom talks

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.801
about how she had anxiety
when you told her

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:19.434
that you wanted to share the
same profession that she had.

00:05:19.434 --> 00:05:20.834
And it's funny
because I had nothing,

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:22.234
you know,
I shouldn't say nothing.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:23.767
I had a lot of anxiety
in my career.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.400
I've had so much more joy
than anxiety.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.968
But if my kids said
they wanted to do it,

00:05:26.968 --> 00:05:28.434
I would have the same thing.

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:30.834
Weirdly, I would think about the
downside more than the upside.

00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:32.300
-Yeah.
-Was that the first time

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.501
she had shared with you that she
was worried when you said

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:35.701
you wanted this path?
-I mean,

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:38.667
at the very beginning
she was very reticent.

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:41.501
But I was a kid.
I mean, I did the, you know,

00:05:41.501 --> 00:05:43.133
played an extra
in my mom's movie,

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.834
"Alice Doesn't Live Here
Anymore" with Scorsese.

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:46.834
And I was a 7-year-old.

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:52.200
But I fell in love with this,
for me, incredible team sport.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.334
It was the first time I had sort
of seen a team sport at work.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:58.934
150 people come together
to make one thing,

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:01.968
as you all well know,
on this incredible show.

00:06:01.968 --> 00:06:04.868
You know, it feels so good
when it works, right?

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:07.968
It doesn't always. But anyway.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.968 --> 00:06:09.567
But this always works.

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.234
-Of course.
-So anyway, just so we're clear.

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:12.501
-We keep thinking we're
going to have a night

00:06:12.501 --> 00:06:14.133
where something falls flat.
But yeah, not yet.

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:17.267
-No, no, but it's, you know,

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:20.234
it's so invigorating
and she saw my passion.

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:22.634
But as you said,
she also saw the pitfalls,

00:06:22.634 --> 00:06:25.868
especially as a young girl
of entering an industry

00:06:25.868 --> 00:06:29.300
where she couldn't always
be protective of me.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.100
So she tried to talk me
out of it, but it didn't work.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.434
-And there's a lovely story
about, as an 11-year-old,

00:06:35.434 --> 00:06:37.834
you took steps to further
your own career at a party.

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:40.234
-[ Laughs ]
Yes, it's so ridiculous.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:43.033
But it is true that I was 11
at my mom's friend,

00:06:43.033 --> 00:06:44.734
the actor Bo Hopkins' house.

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:47.267
And I went up to an agent
and I said,

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.234
"My mom won't help me
and I want to be an actor.

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:50.968
Will you let me audition
for you?"

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:53.033
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:55.100
-It's the most Hollywood thing

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.434
to bring your kid
to a party and be like,

00:06:56.434 --> 00:06:58.033
"Now, don't -- If I catch you
talking to agents..."

00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:00.734
-Yeah, stay away from the
agents.

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:02.367
-I have a lot more questions.

00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:04.467
We will be right back with more
from Laura Dern.

